BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5912f30e562e28009a4eaa"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:We Need To Talk About AI: How Do We Shape AI?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6a5912f30e562e28009a4eaa@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about Artificial Intelligence (AI)?\nJoin us to learn 
 about and discuss aspects of this technological innovation.\nThis workshop
  series explores AI's real impacts on jobs\, privacy\, the environment\, a
 nd our communities\, while discussing how we can shape it responsibly. Eac
 h themed 90-minute session allows for small group discussion\, practical t
 akeaways and honest conversation about AI without the hype.\nOct 7 - AI f
 or the Non-Technical\nIn this session\, we tackle the\n"Elephant in the Ro
 om"\n—addressing ethics\, hallucinations\, and the fear of replacement t
 o foster a responsible\n"AI for Good"\nmindset. We demystify the technolog
 y by viewing AI as an\n"intern with a PhD in everything"\n—a tool that m
 asters five specific superpowers\, such as personalization at scale and ra
 pid research\, while leaving the irreplaceable domain of empathy\, intuiti
 on\, and creativity to you.\nOct 14 - How Do We Shape AI?\nAI is already 
 reshaping how we work\, protect our privacy\, and participate in society
 —yet most of us never get a seat at the table in deciding how it's built
  or used. This session creates that space. Equal parts presentation and co
 nversation\,
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Room B/C
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5912f30e562e28009a4eaa
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f1"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Short Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T193000
UID:69de5e272866a5b4884877f1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you interested in joining a book club but are short on tim
 e?  Then join our Short Book Club\, where we read titles that are 250 page
 s or less. Meetings are held on the second Wednesday of each month.\nOur O
 ctober book is\nCrying in H Mart\nby Michelle Zauner\nSummary: Zauner\, o
 f indie band Japanese Breakfast\, presents a memoir about growing up Korea
 n-American\, losing her mother\, and forging her own identity. She tells o
 f growing up the only Asian-American kid at her school in Eugene\, Oregon\
 ; of struggling with her mother's high expectations of her\; of a painful 
 adolescence\; of treasured months spent in her grandmother's tiny apartmen
 t in Seoul\, where she and her mother would bond over heaping plates of fo
 od. As she grew up\, her Koreanness began to feel ever more distant\, even
  as she found the life she wanted to live. Her mother's diagnosis of termi
 nal pancreatic cancer when Michelle was twenty-five forced a reckoning wit
 h her identity and brought her to reclaim the gifts of taste\, language\, 
 and history her mother had given her.\nNew members are most welcome! Pleas
 e inquire at the circulation desk if you would like a copy of the book.
LOCATION:Parliament Street\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69de5e272866a5b4884877f1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a58e693cca66c2f00a3c968"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:Writers Collective of Canada (WCC):  Community Writing Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6a58e693cca66c2f00a3c968@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Experience the transformative power of writing in an inclusive
  space!\nWriters Collective of Canada (WCC) workshops are unlike any other
 . We write together\, share first drafts in an encouraging environment\, l
 isten deeply to one another\, and receive supportive feedback. Tap into yo
 ur story and explore your voice in an environment of dignity\, courage\, a
 nd respect. (wcc-cec.org)\nNo experience required. Beginners are welcome. 
 Materials are provided.\nWorkshops are not sequential\;\nevery workshop is
  unique. Join ANY session and let’s write together!\nSpace is limited\n.
 \nRegistration required. Call us at 416-393-7683 for more information.
LOCATION:Wychwood\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a58e693cca66c2f00a3c968
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
