BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"UofT Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-annual-toronto-graduate-philosophy-conference/", "University of Toronto": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-annual-toronto-graduate-philosophy-conference/"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-annual-toronto-graduate-philosophy-conference/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:University of Toronto, UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261016
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTSTAMP:20261001T135809Z
CREATED:20260826T185031Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261001T135809Z
UID:35561-1792108800-1792281599@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:2026 Annual Toronto Graduate Philosophy Conference
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the 25th Annual Toronto Graduate Philosophy Conference with keynote speakers Don Garrett (NYU) and Alva Noë (University of California\, Berkeley). \nDon J. Garrett is Silver Professor of Philosophy at New York University. He specializes in early modern philosophy\, with particular interests in the philosophy of David Hume\, Baruch Spinoza\, and Mary Shepherd\, and in areas including epistemology\, metaphysics\, philosophy of mind\, and ethics. He is the author of Cognition and Commitment in Hume’s Philosophy (1997)\, Hume (2015)\, and Nature and Necessity in Spinoza’s Philosophy (2018)\, and editor of The Cambridge Companion to Spinoza (1996)\, and has also published numerous articles on early modern philosophy and related topics in leading academic journals. \nAlva Noë is Professor of Philosophy and Chair of the Department of Philosophy at the University of California\, Berkeley. His research focuses on consciousness\, perception\, the philosophy of mind\, and the philosophy of art. He is the author of Action in Perception (2004)\, Out of Our Heads: Why You Are Not Your Brain and Other Lessons from the Biology of Consciousness (2009)\, Strange Tools: Art and Human Nature (2015)\, and The Entanglement: How Art and Philosophy Make Us What We Are (2023)\, among other books. He has published extensively in academic journals and also writes for a broader public\, with essays appearing in venues including The New York Times and The Atlantic. \nDAY 1: Friday\, October 16 – Room 100 \n\n\n\n9:30am – 10:00am\nBreakfast and welcome\n\n\n10:00am – 11:10am\n“Civic Virtue and Personal Virtue in Aristotle’s Politics” – Xiaoyue Jin\, University of Western Ontario\nChair: Samuel Boudreau\nCommentator: Sebastian Hayden\n\n\n11:10am – 12:10pm\n“Building the Conatus: Spinoza’s Free Man and the Power of Rational Ideas” – Kam-Ho Chan\, University of Massachusetts Amherst\nChair: Raphaelle Dupont\nCommentator: Hojo Liu\n\n\n1:35pm – 2:35pm\n“Asking for Ourselves: Recognition of Other Reasoners in Kant” – Sally Atkins\, University of Oxford\nChair: Alex Drusda\nCommentator: Noa Buckle\n\n\n2:45pm – 3:45pm\n“Hegelian Retributivism: An Approach to Restorative Justice” – Alexia Cipriani\, McMaster University\nChair: Alex Drusda\nCommentator: Faisal K. Bhabha\n\n\n4:00pm – 6:00pm\nKeynote Address:\n“Hume’s Final Verdict on Skepticism: Evidence and the Correction of Doubts in An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding”\nDon J. Garrett\, New York University\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDAY 2: Saturday\, October 17 – Room 100 \n\n\n\n9:30am — 10:00am\nBreakfast\n\n\n10:00am — 11:00am\n“How (Not) to Disagree About What’s Real” – Will Dempsey\, University of California Berkeley\nChair: Gerald Teng\nCommentator: Yi-Cheng Lin\n\n\n11:10am — 12:10pm\n“Reference Magnetism and Arbitrary Reference” – Shay Eizenstadt\, University of Oxford\nChair: Gerald Teng\nCommentator: Patrick Fraser\n\n\n1:35pm — 2:35pm\n“Ontic Oppression of Animals” – María Grazia Sibille\, Virginia Tech\nChair: Eirini Martsoukaki\nCommentator: Jasmine Tremblay D’Ettore\n\n\n2:45pm — 3:45pm\n“Varieties of Denotation in Consent” – Aaron Suduiko\, University of Southern California\nChair: Eirini Martsoukaki\nCommentator: Nate Oppel\n\n\n4:00pm — 6:00pm\nKeynote Address:\n“Consciousness in Love”\nAlva Noë\, University of California Berkeley
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-annual-toronto-graduate-philosophy-conference/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 100 (Main Floor Lecture Hall)\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George,UTM,UTSC
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Grad-Conf-Banner-for-Website-resized.png
END:VEVENT
