SUMMARY:The Information Battle: Elections\, Journalism and Misinformation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T193000
UID:6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:As elections take place in an increasingly complicated online 
 world\, voters are exposed to a huge mix of news\, social media posts\, AI
 -generated content\, and false information spread by organized groups. New
  digital technologies have made it easier for people to learn about issues
  and take part in civic life\, but they have also raised concerns about tr
 ust\, fair elections\, and respectful public discussion.\nThis panel bring
 s together experts in misinformation\, journalism\, and election administr
 ation to explore the challenges and opportunities facing democracy today. 
 How are AI and new technologies changing the way people get information? W
 hat do these changes mean for voters\, journalists\, and election official
 s? And what can be done to build public trust in elections while helping p
 eople stay informed and engaged in public life?\nFeaturing\nNick Hune-Brow
 n\nJournalist and Author\nAward-winning magazine writer and Executive Edit
 or at The Local\nValérie Kindarji\nPolitical scientist\nUniversity of Tor
 onto focused on the impacts of disinformation on democracies in Europe and
  North America and Lab Manager at the University of British Columbia.\nTob
 ias Novogrodsky\nDeputy City Clerk\nElection Services\, City of Toronto\nM
 oderated by:\nAnita Li\nCEO and editor-in-chief of The Green Line\nToronto
 's municipal election takes place on October 26\, 2026. Check out the\nKno
 wYourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebrate Democracy\npage to learn more abo
 ut the upcoming election\, access important voter resources\, and discover
  activities and interactive library programs for all ages happening at a b
 ranch near you.\nThis initiative is made possible by donations to the Toro
 nto Public Library Foundation and is generously supported by the McConnell
  Foundation.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on 
 this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libr
 aries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only 
 be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Ques
 tions about how the library handles your information can be directed to An
 swerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\,
  Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:AI for Job Hunting: Build Your Own System
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T200000
UID:6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Job hunting is a job in itself: applications\, deadlines\, int
 erviews\, follow-ups. What if you could build your own AI-powered system t
 o help you manage it?\nIn this hands-on class with our Innovator-in-Reside
 nce\,\nRyan Kelln\n\, you'll explore a job-hunting dashboard built with AI
 : how it tracks applications\, sets automatic alerts for postings that mat
 ch you\, and practices interviews with you through role-play. You'll leave
  with the templates to build your own. We'll also talk about how employers
  screen applications\, and how it can make things unfair. Come explore a t
 ool that works for you\, not the other way around.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\
 , keyboarding and internet skills. Computers are provided. No coding exp
 erience required. If you wish to save your work from this hands-on progra
 m\, please bring your own USB stick.\nRegistration is required and will st
 art September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your spot will not be guaranteed
  after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAfter this time\, remaining
  seats will be filled on a first come\, first serve basis until capacity i
 s reached.\nAbout the AI Tools Used in This Program\nThis program includes
  activities that use agentic AI tools (listed below) on library laptops.\n
 You do not need to create your own account for this program.\nParticipants
  are encouraged to follow the Resident’s guidance throughout sessions\, 
 which include best practices on using AI.\nWe encourage you to review the 
 privacy statements of each tool prior to attending this program:\nClaude C
 ode\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nClaude Cowork\nLear
 n more about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nGoogle Antigravity\nLearn mo
 re about the tool\nReview privacy policy\nGoogle AI Studio\nLearn more abo
 ut the tool\nReview privacy policy\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pe
 rsonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library
  under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The 
 information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliver
 y and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information
  can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-598
 1\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
