CATEGORIES:Advancement/Alumni,Faculty of Graduate Studies,LA&amp\;PS Addre
 ssing Anti-Black Racism,LA&amp\;PS Advancement,LA&amp\;PS Alumni,LA&amp\;P
 S Department of Anthropology,LA&amp\;PS Department of Equity Studies,LA&am
 p\;PS Department of History,LA&amp\;PS Department of Humanities,LA&amp\;PS
  Department of Languages, Literatures &amp\; Linguistics,LA&amp\;PS Depart
 ment of Politics,LA&amp\;PS Department of Social Science,LA&amp\;PS Depart
 ment of Sociology,LA&amp\;PS Founders College,LA&amp\;PS McLaughlin Colleg
 e,LA&amp\;PS New College,LA&amp\;PS Open to the Public,LA&amp\;PS School o
 f Gender, Sexuality and Women's Studies,LA&amp\;PS School of Public Policy
  &amp\; Administration,LA&amp\;PS School of Social Work,LA&amp\;PS Student
 s All,LA&amp\;PS Students-Graduate,LA&amp\;PS Students-Undergrad,LA&amp\;P
 S Vanier College
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20260821
DESCRIPTION:Immigration posed a difficult question for interwar Canadian
  socialists. One the one hand\, they promoted a universalist vision of a C
 ooperative Commonwealth\; on the other\, they wished to protect Canadian w
 orkers from foreign competition. This lecture explores how social democrat
 s like J.S. Woodsworth\, Agnes Macphail and A.A. Heaps navigated these tu
 rbulent waters.\nSpeaker: Edward Dunsworth\nRegister (In-Person)\nRegister
  (Online)\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20260921
LOCATION:Senate Chamber - N940 Ross Building @ York University\, Toronto\,
  ON M3J\, Canada
ORGANIZER;CN="Abril Liberatori":MAILTO:lapscoms@yorku.ca
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
