CREATED:20260729T135700Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>Title: <b>From Galaxy Catalogs to the Initial Conditions: Bu
 ilding a Digital Twin of the Nearby Universe</b></p>\n\n<p>Abstract: In our
  current cosmological paradigm\, every observable structure\, from the Milk
 y Way to massive galaxy clusters\, grew from a specific realization of prim
 ordial gravitational-potential fluctuations. Can we infer the realization t
 hat gave rise to the galaxies we observe? And can we evolve it forward to o
 btain a physical "digital twin" of our cosmic neighborhood?\n\nModern surve
 ys\, including Rubin-LSST\, Euclid and DESI\, are now mapping millions to b
 illions of galaxies. However\, cosmological analyses still rely mostly on t
 wo-point statistics\, discarding much of the small-scale information. Field
 -level inference\, developed over the past fifteen years\, follows a differ
 ent route: it forward-models the full three-dimensional galaxy field and sa
 mples the primordial fluctuations directly.\n\nIn this talk\, I will presen
 t the Manticore digital twins\, inferred with the BORG (Bayesian Origin Rec
 onstruction from Galaxies) framework. Manticore-Local is built from the 2M+
 + galaxy compilation. Manticore-Deep adds the Sloan Digital Sky Survey (SDS
 S) to reach redshift 0.7 in a 4 Gpc/h box. A Local Group sibling focuses on
  the Milky Way and M31. I will first explain what goes into these reconstru
 ctions and where the models fall short. I will then show how we validate th
 e twins against independent data\, such as cluster masses and galaxy distan
 ces. Finally\, I will discuss the new answers and new problems emerging fro
 m them: the impact on local measurements of the Hubble constant\, the mass 
 of the Local Group\, and new challenges for the subgrid models of hydrodyna
 mical simulations such as the FLAMINGO suite.</p>\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261002T145347Z
LOCATION:MP1318A
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Guilhem Lavaux (University of Waterloo)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
